WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.370
It's great to welcome Neil Ferguson to the
program today.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:08.860
He's a historian and senior fellow at the
Hoover Institution whose new PBS series Net

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.780
World explores the history and science of
networks.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.400
Neil, it's so great to have you on.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.530
I appreciate it.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.099
Thank you, David.

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:18.160
Great to be with you.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:25.019
So to start with, as as a historian or an
historian, as different people love to argue

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.039
about, that's not what I want to ask you about.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:34.930
But as a historian, is the idea that things
have then surely always sort of go back to

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:40.460
feeling the way we remember them feeling is
an inevitability?

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:46.949
Or could we really now be in sort of a situation
that marks a before and after that will be

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:50.730
important in the lives of people who are living
right now?

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:58.190
Well, yeah, that's a great question to ask
an historian or a historian.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:03.850
And in London, it's easy as an historian because
you don't say the H age, right?

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.930
It's a good question to ask historian, because
really we are in a position to look at past

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:17.860
pandemics and see the extent to which they
they changed the way people lived and thought

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.860
we forget.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.680
I suspect how frequent pandemics were in the
past and how exposed populations words are

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.020
contagious lethal diseases.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:34.960
For example, in the 19th century, cholera
regularly swept through major cities on both

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.140
sides of the Atlantic, but particularly in
Europe.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.969
If you go back a little further, smallpox
was the problem that people had to deal with

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:48.140
in their early American colonies and even
subsequently.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.850
And if you want to go all the way back to
the Middle Ages, you had the dreadful problem

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.039
of bubonic and pneumonic plague, which we
call the Black Death.

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:06.720
I think it's fair to say that these great
pandemics with really high mortality did change

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.829
things.

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:14.860
That is to say, people altered their behavior
and they they didn't interact in the way they

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.000
had before for some time subsequently.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
If you read Daniel Defoe on the Great London
Plague of 16 65, he talks about the ways in

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.370
which people had to become less trusting of
even people that they they knew.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.120
And there's an interesting piece of research
on the long lasting impacts of the 1918 19

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.930
influenza pandemic, which really seems to
have affected trust in those countries that

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.319
were most badly affected by the pandemic.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:48.970
On the other hand, if you look at what we're
going through now, it's not as bad a pandemic

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:49.970
as those.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.819
It's not likely to kill as large a share of
the population as, say, the Black Death, which

00:02:53.819 --> 00:03:00.050
killed perhaps a third of humanity, and even
the influenza pandemic of 1918, 19 killed

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.660
between two and three percent of people.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.380
This is not going to be by any means as disastrous.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.720
And so we probably won't be as as strongly
impacted.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.780
Actually, I think the economic measures we've
taken, in particular the lockdown's, will

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:19.240
probably have had a bigger impact than the
disease itself, precisely because the disease

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.340
is so heavily concentrated on certain groups.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.190
It's so much more likely to kill elderly people
than the young, which makes it very different

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.620
from other pandemics.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:34.780
So to cut a very long story short, I would
say the best analogy might actually be with

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:40.170
a recent pandemic which operated in different
ways but killed a great many people.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:46.879
HIV AIDS, HIV AIDS changed the way people
behaved sexually and I think covered 19 will

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:53.900
change the way people behave socially in somewhat
similar ways, especially if there's no vaccine

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.319
in a reasonably short time frame.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:57.280
And that's a big and crucial variable.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.700
If there is a vaccine, if it's rapidly discovered,
if it works really well.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.519
And it's generally available this time next
year, then I'm not sure that our behavior

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:08.090
will radically change over the long term.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:12.780
But if that vaccine doesn't turn up, then
I think you can see big changes in social

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.350
behavior, somewhat similar to the changes
in sexual behavior that came after HIV AIDS.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:21.931
So you mentioned trust, and I'm interested
in hearing a little bit about, you know, when

00:04:21.931 --> 00:04:29.300
we think about the effect of networks and
networks in which we are participants or involved

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.930
in, so to speak.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.860
What is your sense of the factors that most
are influencing, the individual decisions

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.510
people are making about?

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:43.980
Do I go out in protest or not against the
lockdown's or against police brutality?

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.310
Do I wear a mask or don't I wear a mask, et
cetera?

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.670
Is it about who is in your personal network?

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:53.590
Is it about the news you consume?

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.690
Is it about what the CDC says?

00:04:55.690 --> 00:05:01.890
If you are part of a certain network but not
part of another with your sense of that.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:02.890
That's a great question.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:09.570
David, the way I think about this is that
there are really two pandemics going on concurrently.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.910
One is the biological pandemic where a new
corona virus source called two spreads around

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:20.800
the world very rapidly and creates all kinds
of dislocations as well as excess mortality.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.450
But there's this other pandemic of information,
disinformation, misinformation that is going

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.380
on online.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:35.270
And in some ways, some of that contagion is
even more rapid and it spread an extensive

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:36.270
spread.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:41.100
You're actually more likely to encounter that
viral nonsense about Kov at 19, then covered

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.980
19 itself because the second pandemic spreads
much more rapidly.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:54.140
And you can catch a wrong idea from the Internet,
which which you can't do with the virus.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.570
It requires you to be in the same physical
space as a carrier.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:03.770
So I think the second pandemic is really interesting
because it makes a huge difference to your

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:09.710
experience and your assessment of risk, how
you are networks to the rest of the world.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:17.080
If you are the kind of person who gets information
from mainstream media who perhaps is quite

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.800
selective in what you read.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:27.000
Perhaps you are dip into quality newspapers
in more than one country and carefully filter

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.790
roads, click bait.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:35.270
Then you'll form the view, as I would say
I have, that this pandemic ain't over.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:41.090
And if you are particularly in an older age
group, you should not be rushing into crowded

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:43.480
situations, even if they're outdoors.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:49.300
But if your somebody who gets information
from a very different set of sources, perhaps

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:55.830
you're very exposed to the kind of influences
on the far left or the far right who are very

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:00.690
likely to circulate misinformation and disinformation.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:06.400
You might form the conclusion either that
this thing is over and we should just be getting

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.270
right back to normal, which is certainly a
widespread view in some quarters.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:17.460
Or you might think, well, it's still an issue,
but I should be much more outraged about social

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:23.300
justice questions and therefore I should protest
because somehow or other, the virus is going

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.190
to give me a break if I'm doing something
virtuous.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:31.630
So it depends very heavily where you are in
a world where information is no longer mainly

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:37.550
distributed by terrestrial television stations,
as it was, say, back in the 60s, a world in

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:43.380
which information is channeled through very
complex networks in which influences some

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:48.000
of whom you may never even have heard of,
have enormous numbers of followers and can

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:55.070
very quickly persuade them of something that
is in fact quite, quite untrue about the nature

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:56.760
of the virus.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:02.650
So in thinking about some of these different
ideas and conclusions that people may integrate

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.400
into their beliefs, again, depending on the
networks in which they participate, depending

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.250
on the media that they consume.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:12.320
I want to try to link this back to something
I discussed with Jonathan Hite when he was

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:17.590
on the program, which related to sometimes
it seems as though people may vote or act

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.970
against their own personal interests.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:26.970
And one of the ideas that Jonathan High and
others have introduced is that while it that

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:33.140
may be true, what may may not matter to a
greater degree is how much their actions fit

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:39.530
into the interests that whatever orthodoxy
or group they subscribe to has determined

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:40.570
is what's good for them.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:45.230
A good example would be the Tea Party in the
US in 2010, where you had individuals seemingly

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:50.120
supporting a policy that would not be particularly
good for them because they weren't rich.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.130
But it was still I don't know that I want
isn't there rational?

00:08:54.130 --> 00:09:02.210
But we could understand the behavior better
if we understood how group motivations played

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.240
a role.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:10.001
Does that apply to who is doing the protesting
against Lockdown's versus who is and who is

00:09:10.001 --> 00:09:15.800
wearing a mask over versus who isn't this
sort of group analysis rather than individual?

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:21.150
Well, people have been thinking about these
questions for a long time.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:25.970
Antonio Brumskine came up with the notion
of false consciousness when he was trying

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:33.810
to explain the failure of the proletarians
to line up in support of Marxism as an ideology.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:39.340
And, of course, it was a recurrent feature
of European and American history in the 19th

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:47.430
and 20th centuries that working class people
stubbornly refused to support socialist parties.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:52.380
So this is quite an old problem in social
science, especially for the United States,

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:58.270
where socialism was a sort of nonevent until
relatively recently and attracted very little

00:09:58.270 --> 00:09:59.270
support.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:04.510
I think if you if you're asking do people
act against their own individual self interest

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:09.970
because of an ideology or because of a sense
of a wider community?

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:12.340
The answer is yes, they do.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:14.470
Again and again and again.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:20.310
And that's partly because of the appeals that
can be made, whether it's to class identity

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.570
or racial identity or your ethnic identity.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.670
These appeals are quite potent.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:32.330
And by contrast, it's always a little harder
than it looks to establish exactly what your

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:36.240
own self-interest is in economics.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.310
For many years.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:43.420
We tried to imagine a rational, calculating
machine, homo economicus, whose goal was just

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.800
to maximize utility.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:50.820
But as historians, we struggled to find these
people that really rather unusual and and

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.370
indeed not very popular when they do turn
up.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:58.770
People like that exists, but you tend to find
them running hedge funds rather than being

00:10:58.770 --> 00:10:59.800
your everyman.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:06.650
So every man is quite inclined to be swayed
by collective consciousness, by ideas of group

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:11.250
loyalty when it comes down to behavior in
the pandemic.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:16.030
I think there are two distinct forms of I'll
call it irrational behavior.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:22.860
There's the form that says even although the
number of cases and hospitalizations is going

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:27.980
are going up in my state, I'm still going
to go back to work and I'm going to go back

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:33.340
to the stores and I'm not going to wear a
mask because the bells of hell go ting a ling

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.540
ling for you, but not for me.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:40.820
In other words, people essentially don't think
they personally are going to get the virus.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:44.820
Now, that may be because they're young and
they say to themselves, well, I've I've read

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.850
that it really only kills the elderly and
therefore my risk is pretty low.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.930
But what's odd is that we find quite a lot
of older people who say in surveys that they're

00:11:53.930 --> 00:12:00.500
in favor of rapid re-opening even before the
pandemics under control and in their area

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:04.680
on the side of people who recently protested.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:08.680
It might be said that outdoors it's much harder
to catch.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.830
Covered 19 than indoors and the most protests
are outdoors.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:13.650
Therefore, there's not much risk.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:18.300
But when you look at the crowds that pretty
densely packed and the shouting and we know

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:24.900
from the literature that the key to transmission
of source code, too, is it's not just talking

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:28.660
in a normal way, it's shouting and singing
that really along with coughing and sneezing

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.720
that propels the virus from a carrier to others.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.880
So there's some risk involved in going to
these protests.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:40.710
Most of the protesters are relatively young,
but I saw Mitt Romney out in the streets yesterday

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.800
wearing a mask prudently, but it's hard to
tell.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.020
I've just looked at many shots and video clips.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:51.779
I wouldn't say a majority of protesting people
have been wearing masks, and I wouldn't say

00:12:51.779 --> 00:12:55.640
that they're all, as it were, young enough
not to worry.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:00.590
In any case, even if you are young enough
not to be too worried about cough to you,

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:04.650
you should be worrying about the older people
that you're going to meet subsequent to your

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:05.650
protest.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.430
Had a conversation, my daughter, about this
last night.

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:12.630
She's distinctly to the left of me politically,
but she had wisely resisted peer group pressure

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:19.380
to go on a march in London on the ground that
she would soon be seeing her mother and grandmother

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.110
who are in the vulnerable age group.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:27.410
And so that that seemed like a pretty good
example of somebody coming to a good decision,

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:32.670
even under peer group pressure and peer group
pressures, a huge part of what's going on

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:33.670
here.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:38.220
People may suspect they're doing something
that's not prudent, but they kind of give

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.970
in to the peer group pressure.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.779
I was glad my daughter resisted that.

00:13:42.779 --> 00:13:49.860
When it comes to beliefs about blame and responsibility
around the virus, there's definitely some

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:51.820
interesting network effects going on.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:55.660
I was interested to see in your recent interview
with Andy Server.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:58.680
I'll let you sort of characterize in detail
your position.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:04.790
But at least generally speaking, you seem
to probably assign less responsibility to

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.560
the federal government than Trump's actions
than I would.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:14.850
Although I do believe that there have been
unfair accusations also cast that the federal

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:19.790
government, which were either beyond anyone's
control or more relevant to a mayor or a governor,

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.730
for example.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:27.360
What do you think are the unfair criticisms
that are or blame that is being placed on

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.360
the president?

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.060
Maybe we can start there?

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:36.279
Well, I think it's worth say right away that
the primary responsibility for this pandemic

00:14:36.279 --> 00:14:42.120
lies with the Chinese governments and also
the provincial authorities in Hubei province

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:50.290
who really criminally failed to deal with
the problem and were slow to admit the extent

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:53.220
to which human to human transmission was happening.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:57.460
I agree, Laide imposing travel bans until
it was really much too late.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:58.860
So that's part one.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:04.010
Part two of the answer is that the media obsession
with President Trump, which he of course encourages,

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:11.490
led, I think, to a tendency to overemphasize
the president's role in reality in a crisis

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.029
of this sors.

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:18.820
The agencies that really matter are those
responsible for pandemic preparedness.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:25.730
And that is a part of the Department of Health
and Human Services, not just CDC, which has

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:26.860
got a lot of the stake.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:35.230
But we have an undersecretary or I think an
assistant secretary, to be exact, at DHHS,

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:40.130
whose job this is and who had written and
spoken a good deal on the subject.

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:46.279
In fact, if you delve into it, the federal
bureaucracy had generated any number of reports

00:15:46.279 --> 00:15:50.850
on pandemic preparedness and PowerPoint decks
with multiple slides.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:55.970
And indeed, on paper, the United States was
well-prepared for a situation like this.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:01.170
It was just that when it actually happened
back in January and right through February

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:08.100
and into March, that the agencies responsible,
the people whose job this was, did terribly.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:12.860
And we can say that I think fairly, because
we can see where it was done much better in

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:18.050
countries where public health bureaucracies
were quick to respond, like in, for example,

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:20.440
Taiwan or South Korea or Germany, for that
matter.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:22.660
You don't just need to look at Asian countries.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:28.120
So the U.S. in common with I'd say also the
United Kingdom did really badly dealing with

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:31.339
a problem they were supposed to be ready for.

00:16:31.339 --> 00:16:36.100
Making it all about Trump, I think, is a mistake
which misunderstands the nature of government.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:42.279
It is not that the president sits there in
the White House with his phone ready to tweet,

00:16:42.279 --> 00:16:43.990
deciding on everything.

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:45.380
That's just not the way it works.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:50.000
The federal government's a hugely complex
thing with multiple agencies, more than 60

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:51.350
in total.

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:57.040
And there is an agency who has this job and
they failed and not nearly enough has been

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:58.040
written about.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.040
That's in my view.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:00.040
This is not to say Trump did great.

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:01.300
He did quite badly.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:03.300
His decision making was erratic.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:06.920
He had some good instincts, like we have to
do something about people coming in from China.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:10.780
But it came much too late to make any difference
at all at the end of January.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:15.220
And I think instinctively Trump felt that
Lockdown's were a mistake, which I think was

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:18.720
right, too, because it was an incredibly blunt
instrument to deal with.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:24.040
The contagion are not necessary if, in fact,
you've done the right things early on.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.679
So, you know, you can't say it's all his fault.

00:17:26.679 --> 00:17:28.370
I think that's a great mistake.

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:29.850
But nor can you say he's done a great job.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:34.740
I mean, in the end, the buck stops with the
president, the United States, as Harry Truman

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.220
said long ago.

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:37.940
And so one can't say what it was.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.910
All the faults of DHHS, not the president's
fault.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:42.050
He's the president.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.030
Ultimately, the buck stops with him.

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:48.140
There's no question that the United States
has handled this pandemic very much worse

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:51.760
than it should have, very much worse than
other comparable countries.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:56.390
And that is why we have the highest death
toll and may well end up with a very high

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:59.510
excess mortality rate by the end of the year.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:01.230
So maybe we're not actually that far.

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:06.100
I mean, in hearing you laid out, it seems
like some of this is about the president as

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:12.550
the, you know, HHS run by Alex Azar, who early
on thought it was a good idea to put a former

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:16.240
labradoodle dealer in charge of the day to
day.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:18.900
OK, that was a bad decision.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.510
He is Trump's pick to run HHS.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:27.800
So a lot of this seems to be the semantics
of Trump's delegation versus Trump's actions.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:34.250
I mean, I think the biggest critique certainly
would be when you look at South Korea and

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:38.330
the US finding our first cases within hours
of each other.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:41.660
South Korea, rapid test development fever.

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:47.140
Lennix isolation, contact tracing that happened
six weeks later.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:51.500
Or it started to happen in a serious way,
six weeks later, the testing capacity was

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:52.500
a joke.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:57.220
I do think, you know, you can't blame Trump
for one hundred thousand one hundred ten thousand

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:58.220
deaths.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:03.470
Of course, there was probably a period there,
I would say, around, you know, aggressively

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:09.731
March 10th, less aggressively, March 25th,
where the request for a coordinated 50 state

00:19:09.731 --> 00:19:14.380
stay at home, that would be long over by now
had it been done from the start.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:18.820
And we wouldn't have what we now have rolling,
opening and closing.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.550
Florida and Texas spiking.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.510
Well, fortunately, here in Massachusetts,
we're doing well.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.540
That certainly was a lost opportunity for
Trump.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:30.110
And I would even say it was a lost opportunity
from a campaign standpoint.

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:35.920
I think that he has now suffered greatly because
he didn't do some of these things that may

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:40.170
have been economically unpopular at first,
but would likely have him with a much higher

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.140
than 38 percent approval today.

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:45.790
I wonder if you agree it's a difficult.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:52.140
I'm not I mean, my sense is that by mid-March
it was it was too late.

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:58.770
And that blanket shelter in place orders would
not really have had a huge impact by that

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:03.780
stage other than, in fact, to magnify the
economic shock.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:09.049
Let me reiterate, the key decision points
were in January, as soon as it became clear

00:20:09.049 --> 00:20:10.049
there was a problem.

00:20:10.049 --> 00:20:11.049
Yeah.

00:20:11.049 --> 00:20:15.760
And right into February, when President Trump
and many others in the administration were

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:19.320
downplaying the threat, not only President
Trump.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:24.190
Remember, at the end of January, when Trump
called for a travel ban on Chinese passengers

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:29.670
coming into the U.S., he was widely criticized
in the liberal media accused essentially of

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:30.670
racism.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:36.900
And there were multiple articles at that time
in liberal circles saying it's just the flu.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:38.870
In fact, you should worry much more about
the flu.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:40.920
And for me, as somebody who was writing.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.400
No, this really isn't the flu.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:43.980
This is very much more serious.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:48.870
It was maddening to see such ill informed
rubbish being published so widely.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:54.980
There was a bipartisan failure to recognize
the scale of the threat that went on right

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:55.980
in February.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:21:00.410
I can remember being in Washington in mid-February
trying to convince people in the administration

00:21:00.410 --> 00:21:01.549
this thing is real.

00:21:01.549 --> 00:21:05.350
You have got to do something and you particularly
have to tell the president to stop saying

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:09.390
there is no threat to the United States is
just going to disappear.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:15.230
So it was it was then that I think the really
disastrous mistakes were made by mid-March.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:20.080
They had been insouciant for so long that
they were actually thrown into panic by another

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:21.080
Neil Ferguson.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.140
It's worth clarifying that I'm not him any.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:27.740
I'll Ferguson, the epidemiologist at Imperial
College London, who published a report on

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:32.250
March 16, saying that if you didn't have Lockdown's
two point two million people were going to

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:34.750
die in the United States and half a million
in the UK.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:40.559
And both the London and Washington governments
flipped when they saw this, as did many people

00:21:40.559 --> 00:21:42.490
who saw the report or read about it.

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:46.059
And we went from total Insu Saelens to total
panic.

00:21:46.059 --> 00:21:50.059
This was really the worst of both worlds because
we had dithered around when we should have

00:21:50.059 --> 00:21:52.010
been taking early action.

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:55.930
And then we did a bunch of extremely blunt
instrument measures like shelter in place,

00:21:55.930 --> 00:22:02.510
orders and economic lockdowns that it doesn't
seem to me have radically changed the spread

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:05.059
or slowed the spread of the virus.

00:22:05.059 --> 00:22:10.330
Social distancing was something that mattered
a lot when people adopted that, even not at

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:13.380
the order of the state had a big impact.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:17.350
The lockdowns have clearly done much more
economic harm than public health.

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:18.350
Good.

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:22.580
So, as I said, we've ended up with this kind
of worst of both worlds and it's not over

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:23.580
for me.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:28.920
The depressing thing is seeing people behaving
all over the country as if it is over in in

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:34.610
defiance of the reality that if you just look
at numbers of of confirmed cases, there are

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:37.270
second waves already in about a dozen states.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:38.679
It's already happening.

00:22:38.679 --> 00:22:41.910
And so let me ask you that last maybe idea
that it's over.

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:42.910
Yeah.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:46.950
So the last thing I wanted to touch on was
for a while I've been fearing that there was

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:52.320
going to be a point at which it's going to
be sort of unclear what each person should

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.830
be doing here in Massachusetts today.

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:59.920
Outdoor dining gets to open and then this
is phase two A and then we're going to get

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.840
to phase 2B where something else can happen.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.950
And, you know, I don't know, I'm in my mid
thirties.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:09.880
I I'm probably fine to go and sit outside
at a restaurant, but should I do it?

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:12.370
And is it if you need a mask.

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:15.000
Is this is that a signal that I shouldn't
be doing it?

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.870
Or what do you make of this phase?

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:21.030
Now that could last really it could last until
there's a vaccine.

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:24.000
I don't know how long it's going to last,
where there's going to be a lot of needing

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:28.070
to sort of figure out for oneself what is
the appropriate behavior.

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:34.460
Yes, I think you're right to feel confused
and uncertain because mixed policy signals

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:40.010
at the national and state level have created
exactly that sense of confusion all over the

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:41.010
country.

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:44.120
And this goes back to what we were talking
about earlier, when people are uncertain but

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:52.620
get their information from, let's say, more
sensationalistic sources, but then they will

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:53.820
take action.

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:58.010
That is, in fact, not going to help and might
even harm them.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:04.049
Hydroxyl chloroquine, for example, is an example
of something that was touted as a remedy not

00:24:04.049 --> 00:24:07.110
just by the president, but by many other people.

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:13.060
But the only really good scientific research
on this suggests that it has no real prophylactic

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:17.140
effect, that it's quite harmful if you are
suffering seriously from Kovar 19.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:23.830
So as people try to work out what to do, they're
battling against a combination of poor quality

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:26.230
public information from the government.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:30.880
I mean, I think our public health bureaucracy
has failed at the communications level, by

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:31.880
the way.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:36.340
Really interesting piece on this subject in
The New York Times just about a week ago,

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:39.050
going through what exactly went wrong at CDC.

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:42.690
It's well worth reading, unlike many things
in The New York Times.

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:47.950
And it actually shows a classic pathology
of a Burack bureaucratic organization, very

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:52.860
hierarchical, very slow moving and easily
bullied by the White House.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:58.950
When the going got tough, it is a very, very
stark illustration of a problem that the great

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:04.450
physicist Richard Feynman identified long
ago in the case of the Challenger space shuttle

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:05.450
disaster.

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:09.420
It's the mid-level bureaucracy that's often
the problem in disasters like this.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:14.309
So we as individuals are getting poor information
from the public authorities and even worse

00:25:14.309 --> 00:25:16.280
information from the Internet.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:17.280
What are we going to do?

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:23.210
Well, we have to make sure that we read carefully
what is out there that is reputable.

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:26.080
That is to say, has some solid basis in scientific
research.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:33.490
And on that basis, my advice to you is you
probably find to go and eat outside in Massachusetts

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:34.750
on a nice day.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:40.270
You should wear a mask and you should really
keep your distance from from other people

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:41.940
as far as you can.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:45.450
Just to be on the safe side, because Massachusetts
is a state where there is still quite a high

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:51.200
level of cases of cold 19 being diagnosed
every day.

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:56.630
Would I advise you to go into a crowded office
or to take a crowded form of public transport?

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:59.280
No, I'd say avoid those things.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:06.700
Avoid any crowded indoor space, especially
if people are shouting or singing.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:08.700
So forget choir practice.

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:14.230
And if somebody says, let's have a re-opening
party, come on round to my place, politely

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:19.160
decline because those are very likely the
super spreader events that we're going to

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:23.320
get in the coming weeks and months that will
keep this pandemic going in the United States

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.320
for much longer than was necessary.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:28.390
Seems eminently reasonable.

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:31.870
I would say we've been speaking with Neil
Ferguson.

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:37.970
He is an historian and the new PBS series
Net World explores the history and science

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:38.970
of networks.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:40.840
Neil, really appreciate your time today.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:42.220
Thanks, David.

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:44.220
It's been a pleasure and enjoy that.

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.120
Enjoy that dinner remasters.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:47.940
That's the problem.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:49.320
That's the that's the logistical issue.

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:51.380
I will figure it out.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:52.660
Thank you.

